The internal resistance \(r\) of a cell is the resistance offered by its electrolyte and electrodes to the flow of current inside the cell. It depends on:
1. the distance between the electrodes — \(r\) increases as the electrodes are moved further apart;
2. the area of the electrodes dipped in the electrolyte — \(r\) decreases as this area is increased;
3. the nature and concentration of the electrolyte — a more concentrated electrolyte offers a larger resistance;
4. the temperature — \(r\) decreases as the temperature of the electrolyte rises;
5. the amount of current drawn from the cell and the age of the cell — an old or heavily used cell has a larger internal resistance.
[Note: this chapter shows how \(r\) is MEASURED with a potentiometer, using \(r = R\left(\dfrac{l_1 - l_2}{l_2}\right)\), but it does not itself list the factors above; they belong to the treatment of cells in the earlier standard.]
